+## OpenCode Go integration reference
+
+Use the existing Codenotch provider implementation before rediscovering supplier integration details: `OpenCodeCredentials.swift`, `OpenCodeSchema.swift`, `OpenCodeProvider.swift` and `OpenCodeUsage.swift` at the pinned reference commit above. The selected integration follows its credential-specific route pairing: API keys use `GET https://opencode.ai/zen/go/v1/usage`; OAuth uses `GET https://opencode.ai/inference/go/v1/usage`, with the actual known `x-opencode-org-id`. A newer official OpenCode client also uses the former route for OAuth; that differs from the pinned reference and is not grounds to silently mix contracts or retry credentials across endpoints.
+
+Both remote responses contain `usage.rolling`, `usage.weekly` and `usage.monthly`. Each valid meter supplies used `percent` and ISO-8601 `resetsAt` (fractional seconds are supported); remaining is `1 - percent / 100`, never inferred from local token logs or dollar prices. Preserve valid siblings when another meter is missing or malformed. Rolling is five hours, weekly is seven days, and monthly is a calendar month rather than an assumed thirty days. Unknown reset times remain unknown.
+
+Credentials are read-only from OpenCode's own supported store. Honor its XDG data-root convention, including on Windows; do not substitute AppData or macOS Application Support. API keys, OAuth secrets and raw responses never enter logs, fixtures or account caches. Re-read credential identity on refresh and isolate structured cached snapshots and persisted backoff by provider and account. An account/credential switch must not commit a previous request's result. No independent OAuth renewal or browser-cookie extraction is performed.
+
+Follow the reference's distinction between missing Go entitlement and sign-out: 403 means no Go allowance, not measured zero. On OAuth usage 401, the authenticated Console `/api/user` probe can establish that login is valid but Go is absent; a model-list response cannot establish authentication. Respect persisted Retry-After and failure backoff. Keep stale observations visibly stale with their original timestamps. Real subscription verification requires an accessible authenticated account; deterministic synthetic checks are not live-account proof.
+
+Go follows the pinned provider's polling semantics: successful requests do not create a hard minimum request interval, so a manual refresh may request again after the previous request completes. Automatic application polling remains five minutes; concurrent refreshes are coalesced, and persisted rate-limit/failure retry deadlines remain binding. This does not change Codex's existing successful-request floor or startup rules.
